<section class="av_toggle_section av-mqqlnamu-07223510d1273465a015989c18d6ae25" itemprop="blogPost" itemscope="itemscope" itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><div class="single_toggle" data-tags="{All} " role="tablist"><p aria-controls="toggle-id-1" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Click to expand: 1. What is neuroplasticity?" class="toggler av-title-above" data-aria_collapsed="Click to expand: 1. What is neuroplasticity?" data-aria_expanded="Click to collapse: 1. What is neuroplasticity?" data-fake-id="#toggle-id-1" data-slide-speed="200" data-title="1. What is neuroplasticity?" data-title-open="" id="toggle-toggle-id-1" itemprop="headline" role="tab" tabindex="0">1. What is neuroplasticity?<span class="toggle_icon"><span class="vert_icon"></span><span class="hor_icon"></span></span></p><div aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="toggle-toggle-id-1" class="toggle_wrap av-title-above" id="toggle-id-1" role="region" tabindex="-1"><div class="toggle_content invers-color" itemprop="text"><p>Neuroplasticity is the brain’s ability to rewire, rebuild, and adapt at any age – restructuring itself at the cellular level every time you learn, recover, or challenge your mind meaningfully.</p>
</div></div></div></section>
<section class="av_toggle_section av-mqqlnp30-87a4daad1af10394f2f6decf538daad9" itemprop="blogPost" itemscope="itemscope" itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><div class="single_toggle" data-tags="{All} " role="tablist"><p aria-controls="toggle-id-2" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Click to expand: 2. Does the brain really decline after your twenties?" class="toggler av-title-above" data-aria_collapsed="Click to expand: 2. Does the brain really decline after your twenties?" data-aria_expanded="Click to collapse: 2. Does the brain really decline after your twenties?" data-fake-id="#toggle-id-2" data-slide-speed="200" data-title="2. Does the brain really decline after your twenties?" data-title-open="" id="toggle-toggle-id-2" itemprop="headline" role="tab" tabindex="0">2. Does the brain really decline after your twenties?<span class="toggle_icon"><span class="vert_icon"></span><span class="hor_icon"></span></span></p><div aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="toggle-toggle-id-2" class="toggle_wrap av-title-above" id="toggle-id-2" role="region" tabindex="-1"><div class="toggle_content invers-color" itemprop="text"><p>That story is largely wrong. Modern neuroscience shows the brain keeps creating new connections and adapting well into later life – it’s a flexible, living organ, not a machine that wears out.</p>
</div></div></div></section>
<section class="av_toggle_section av-mqqlnp30-1-98ad890ecb206dfa59ef8528a9ddf1cc" itemprop="blogPost" itemscope="itemscope" itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><div class="single_toggle" data-tags="{All} " role="tablist"><p aria-controls="toggle-id-3" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Click to expand: 3. What changes in the aging brain?" class="toggler av-title-above" data-aria_collapsed="Click to expand: 3. What changes in the aging brain?" data-aria_expanded="Click to collapse: 3. What changes in the aging brain?" data-fake-id="#toggle-id-3" data-slide-speed="200" data-title="3. What changes in the aging brain?" data-title-open="" id="toggle-toggle-id-3" itemprop="headline" role="tab" tabindex="0">3. What changes in the aging brain?<span class="toggle_icon"><span class="vert_icon"></span><span class="hor_icon"></span></span></p><div aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="toggle-toggle-id-3" class="toggle_wrap av-title-above" id="toggle-id-3" role="region" tabindex="-1"><div class="toggle_content invers-color" itemprop="text"><p>Processing speed slows, working memory becomes less efficient, and white matter integrity can decrease. These are real changes – but crystallized intelligence, emotional regulation, and complex reasoning can remain sharp.</p>
</div></div></div></section>
<section class="av_toggle_section av-mqqloqe6-33cbfa47d18fc1c0daa46de255ee0e3e" itemprop="blogPost" itemscope="itemscope" itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><div class="single_toggle" data-tags="{All} " role="tablist"><p aria-controls="toggle-id-4" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Click to expand: 4. Can older adults truly learn new skills?" class="toggler av-title-above" data-aria_collapsed="Click to expand: 4. Can older adults truly learn new skills?" data-aria_expanded="Click to collapse: 4. Can older adults truly learn new skills?" data-fake-id="#toggle-id-4" data-slide-speed="200" data-title="4. Can older adults truly learn new skills?" data-title-open="" id="toggle-toggle-id-4" itemprop="headline" role="tab" tabindex="0">4. Can older adults truly learn new skills?<span class="toggle_icon"><span class="vert_icon"></span><span class="hor_icon"></span></span></p><div aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="toggle-toggle-id-4" class="toggle_wrap av-title-above" id="toggle-id-4" role="region" tabindex="-1"><div class="toggle_content invers-color" itemprop="text"><p>Yes. Synaptic plasticity never stops. A seventy-year-old can become fluent in a new language. Age slows certain processes, but it does not eliminate the brain’s capacity to learn and adapt.</p>
</div></div></div></section>
<section class="av_toggle_section av-mqqlp5is-2a7185b153b8b8bcc0c5f7e02ad8f5d6" itemprop="blogPost" itemscope="itemscope" itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><div class="single_toggle" data-tags="{All} " role="tablist"><p aria-controls="toggle-id-5" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Click to expand: 5. What is synaptic plasticity?" class="toggler av-title-above" data-aria_collapsed="Click to expand: 5. What is synaptic plasticity?" data-aria_expanded="Click to collapse: 5. What is synaptic plasticity?" data-fake-id="#toggle-id-5" data-slide-speed="200" data-title="5. What is synaptic plasticity?" data-title-open="" id="toggle-toggle-id-5" itemprop="headline" role="tab" tabindex="0">5. What is synaptic plasticity?<span class="toggle_icon"><span class="vert_icon"></span><span class="hor_icon"></span></span></p><div aria-hidden="true" aria-labelledby="toggle-toggle-id-5" class="toggle_wrap av-title-above" id="toggle-id-5" role="region" tabindex="-1"><div class="toggle_content invers-color" itemprop="text"><p>Synaptic plasticity is the strengthening or weakening of connections between neurons based on activity. It’s the foundational biological process that enables recovery, learning, and lasting cognitive change at any age.</p>
</div></div></div></section>
